    "content": "A Red Flag Warning is the highest-level alert issued by the National Weather Service for critical fire weather conditions. It indicates that a dangerous combination of high temperatures, very low humidity, and strong, gusty winds is happening or imminent (within 24 hours). These conditions can produce extreme fire behavior, allowing fires to start and spread rapidly. Key Usage Examples & Precautions: Burn Bans: Avoid all outdoor burning, including campfires, fire pits, and charcoal grills. Machinery Use: Avoid mowing dry grass, operating chainsaws, or using welding equipment, as sparks can ignite wildfires. Vehicles: Avoid driving or parking vehicles on dry grass, as exhaust systems can ignite fuel. Fire Prevention: Properly extinguish all smoking materials; do not throw cigarettes out of vehicles. Preparedness: Keep vehicles charged, review evacuation plans, and keep kits handy. Synonyms and Related Terms: Fire Weather Warning Extreme Fire Danger Alert (Commonly used phrasing) Fire Weather Watch (Issued 24-72 hours in advance when conditions are possible) These warnings are most common in the Western U.S. during dry, windy seasons.",
